  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 416 AM MST Tue Nov 24 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nHigh amplitude upper level ridging is building into the region early \nthis morning. Although benign weather is often associated with a \nstrong upper level ridge, there will be a couple initial concerns \nthis morning. Across the plains the initial concern will be for a \nsufficiently strong pressure gradient to result in some blowing and \ndrifting snow near the Northern Rocky Mountain Front. This pressure \ngradient slowly weakens later this morning, with surface \ntemperatures also rising above freezing by afternoon. The \ncombination of these two factors leads to concern for blowing and \ndrifting of snow rapidly decreasing later this morning. \n\nThe other initial concern will be for areas of very low clouds and \ndense fog across Central and Southwest MT valleys. While the winds \nhave developed early this morning across portions of the plains, \nthey have yet to make any inroads into many Central and Southwest MT \nvalleys. Given there has been nothing to mix out these valleys,\nlingering moisture will result in the risk for fog continuing through\nthe remainder of the night and this morning. A Dense Fog Advisory\nhas been issued for this area valid through 10AM this morning. \n\nA series of weakening upper level troughs will move onshore across \nthe west coast later this week, with initial impact being a more \nzonal flow aloft. Gusty winds will become a bit more widespread \nWednesday onward, which will help trend all areas warmer. \nTemperatures largely look to be above freezing when these winds \ndevelop, which helps keep blowing and drifting snow concerns minimal \nWednesday into Thanksgiving. \n\nStronger zonal flow aloft moves overhead Thanksgiving into Friday. \nForecast soundings show the initial concern for strong winds being \nconfined to the Rocky Mountain Front early morning before the \nrisk for stronger winds expands further into the afternoon. While \nprobabilities for gusts in excess of 75 mph are greater than 60% \nalong the eastern slopes of the Rocky Mountain Front for Thursday \ninto Friday, the uncertainty with respect to onset time of these \nstronger winds is resulting in a continuation of the High Wind \nWatch as opposed to an upgrade to a High Wind Warning. \n\nAlong with the stronger mid level winds Thanksgiving into Friday \nwill be a stronger push of Pacific moisture. Most of the \nprecipitation associated with this push looks to remain along and \nnear the Continental Divide through Friday. At this time the \ndominant precipitation looks to be snow over the passes. Those with \npost-Thanksgiving travel plans across the Continental Divide should \nmonitor trends in the forecast. \n\nA northwesterly flow aloft then looks to develop across the region \nthis weekend as upper level ridging builds in off to the west. There\nis quite a bit of uncertainty with respect to both the amplitude \nand positioning of the aforementioned ridge, which leads to lower \nconfidence in specifics over this timeframe. The main takeaway \nwill be for unsettled conditions this weekend with temperatures \ntrending back closer toward average. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nInitial uncertainty is related to how long there will be a blowable \nsnowpack as winds increase over the next couple days over the \nplains. Current thinking is that most areas will warm sufficiently \nto mitigate any longer duration blowing or drifting snow concerns. \n\nLatest snow depth forecast for Thursday morning shows largely less \nthan 2 inches snow depth remaining along the Rocky Mountain Front \nwhere the strongest winds are forecast to develop. This helps limit \nconcerns for any crust breaking and resulting in blowing snow when \nthe strongest winds develop Thursday into Friday. \n\nThe other main source of uncertainty is with respect to \nprecipitation late week and into the weekend. At this time the \nEuropean Extreme Forecast Index is not keying in on any specific day \nto be overly impactful, at least with respect to climatology for \nthis time of the year. This is related to both ensemble spread in \nboth timing and magnitude of precipitation. Trends will be \nmonitored for any increases in confidence for the post- \nThanksgiving timeframe. -AM",
